# Interview Transcript with Adhis

**Q: Hello, hello can you tell me what your name is?**  
A: Adhis  

---

**Q: Who do you usually turn to for advice about course selection and why?**  
A: I usually turn to my seniors because they have already taken the courses and are more experienced, so their advice is usually accurate and honest.  

---

**Q: Would your confidence change if the advice came from someone with the same dual-degree background? Why or why not?**  
A: Honestly, not really, because it doesn’t really matter whether they have a dual degree or not. As long as they have already taken the course, the advice is still useful.  

---

**Q: Okay. How confident would you be, on a scale of 1 to 10, if someone from a dual-degree background gave you advice?**  
A: Maybe eight or nine.  

---

**Q: Do you think it would be helpful to your studies?**  
A: Well, yeah, because it helps me decide which courses I should take.  

---

**Q: Do you think tailoring course options to your preferences, like avoiding finals or group work, would help you make course selections?**  
A: Yeah, I don’t really like exams, so I would most likely choose courses without final exams.  

---

**Q: How much easier do you think it would be, on a scale of 1 to 10?**  
A: Ten.  

---

**Q: Tell me about a time when a course you wanted was full. How did it affect you?**  
A: It really messed up my plan because I had everything scheduled, and then I had to look for other courses to replace it. That took extra time and energy.  

---

**Q: How do you usually figure out alternatives when enrolment doesn’t go as planned?**  
A: I usually search for available courses and also ask my friends and seniors in such situations.  

---

**Q: On a scale of 1 to 10, if you had access to automatic alternatives like summer courses or path changes, would you use it?**  
A: Yeah.  

---

**Q: If so, on a scale of 1 to 10, how helpful do you think it would be in keeping students like you on track?**  
A: Nine.  

---

**Q: Have you ever considered changing your major or minor? If so, what kind of questions or concerns came up during that process?**  
A: Yeah. I wondered about the process, like who I should ask, what requirements I need to prepare, and how it would affect my plan.  

---

**Q: Would you be open to changing your course plan even if you don’t like what you’re studying? If you could safely explore different study paths without affecting your plan, would you use it, and how do you imagine using it?**  
A: Probably just try out courses from other majors. I’d like to see which one matches my interests, and I wouldn’t need to worry about delaying my graduation.  

---

**Q: Last question: on a scale of 1 to 10, how hesitant or scared would you be to change your course path even with the guidance of the automatic simulator?**  
A: Four.  
